AUTHOR MATERIAL
The following documents are the only source of truth. Do not use general knowledge about the subject.

{{ documents }}

WHAT COUNTS AS A REQUIREMENT
A requirement is something the author has stated the finished work must or must not do: a claim it must make, a term it must use or avoid, a length, an audience, a structure, a prohibition. A topic is not a requirement. A subject being interesting is not a requirement. Your own view of what would improve the work is not a requirement.

FOR EACH REQUIREMENT
- text: one sentence, stating what the finished work must do, checkable by reading the work.
- blocking: true when the author stated it as a must or must-not; false when it reads as a preference.
- source_document: the name of the document above that states it, exactly as it is labelled.
- source_quote: at least {{ min_quote_chars }} characters copied WORD FOR WORD from that document. Not a paraphrase. If you cannot copy such a span, do not return the requirement.
- checks: zero or more deterministic checks, each one of:
    {"kind": "must_contain_any", "values": ["..."]}
    {"kind": "must_contain_all", "values": ["..."]}
    {"kind": "must_not_contain", "values": ["..."]}
    {"kind": "min_words", "threshold": 0}
    {"kind": "max_words", "threshold": 0}
    {"kind": "heading_present", "values": ["..."]}
  Give a check only where a literal string or a number really decides it. Where nothing literal decides it, give no checks; that is honest and is recorded as such. There is no pattern or regular-expression check and you may not request one.

RETURN
{"requirements": [ ... ]}
and nothing else. An empty list is a valid answer.
